  [38;5;153msettings.js[39m hands its values to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m determines both the module's shape and whether [38;5;153mindex.html[39m still works when
  opened directly from disk.

  - [1mGlobal via second [38;5;153m<script>[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153msettings.js[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m; it exposes one frozen global (e.g.
    [38;5;153mwindow.AppSettings[39m). Matches the existing plain-script idiom exactly, keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working, zero risk. Costs you
    one more global on [38;5;153mwindow[39m. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m with [38;5;153mimport { apiEndpoint } from './settings.js'[39m. No globals,
    proper module boundary, the direction the ecosystem went. But ES modules are blocked by CORS over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so
    opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking stops working — you'd need a local HTTP server to run the page at all. That's
    a workflow change, not just a syntax change.

  [1mMy recommendation: the global.[0m The repo's whole idiom is plain scripts with no tooling, and ES modules would trade a
  working "open the file" workflow for a boundary that buys little in a two-file page. If the app later grows a build
  step, converting then is mechanical.

  I'm also assuming [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m stay untouched — they're CommonJS Node code with no API endpoint, so
  pulling them into a browser settings module would be scope creep. Say the word if you want them covered too.

